            <div xml:id="d331">

                <pb facs="0381" n="281" sameAs="frus:frus1920v03/pg281"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">856d.6363/16: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in the Netherlands</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Phillips</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >The Hague</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-10-08T13:00:00+00:19"><hi rendition="#italic">October 8,
                                1920—1 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 4:37 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>270. My 269 October 7, 4 p.m. I think it is possible that Bataafsche Company may
                    be induced to allow American capital to participate. <pb facs="0382" n="282"
                        xml:id="pg282"/> I am of course interesting myself only in behalf of
                    American capital in general but am in touch with representative of oil company
                    mentioned in my telegram 237 September [<hi rendition="#italic">August</hi>] 27,
                    6 p.m. who is ready to take advantage of opportunity if the Bataafsche Company
                    should decide in favor of American capital. Is this satisfactory to the
                    Department? A prompt reply would be appreciated.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
